  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nLatest radar indicated that the heavier rainfall is moving off the \ncoast south of Port Aransas, while light rain continues across S TX \nthis afternoon. Generally light rain is expected to continue through \nthe early evening hours. Models are in good agreement with another \nround of heavy rainfall tonight into Monday morning. This is in \nresponse to another embedded short wave trough tracking northeast \nahead of a cut off mid level low that is currently centered over W \nTX and northern Mexico. The low is providing large scale ascent \nacross the region, while a surface trough that is meandering across \nS TX is providing low level moisture convergence. Combined with the \ndeep moisture across the area, this setup is conducive for \nadditional heavy rainfall. An additional 2-4 inches with isolated 5-\n6 inches will be possible tonight through Monday, mainly across the \nsouthern Brush Country to southern Coastal Bend. This additional \nrain on top of saturated soil from the earlier rain, will increase \nthe risk of flash flooding. Models indicate that the heavier \nrain/showers and thunderstorms will move across the Brush Country \nthis evening and the remainder of S TX overnight through Monday \nmorning. The Victoria Crossroads will also have additional rain \ntonight and Monday and are in a Marginal Risk for excessive \nrainfall. Additional rainfall amounts across the Crossroads are \nexpected to range from 0.5-1.5 inches. Can not rule out higher \namounts around 2-3 inches.\n\nRain chances decrease to a medium chance Monday afternoon. Low to \nmedium rain chances expected Tuesday and Wednesday. The potential \nfor a cold front by Thursday could bring drier and cooler conditions \nto south TX the latter part of the week.\n\nAnother concern is high risk of rip currents and minor coastal \nflooding continuing on Monday. The chance of minor coastal flooding \nalong gulf facing beaches will increase once again Monday during \nhigh tide. This is due to a strengthening east-northeast flow across \nthe gulf waters tonight into Monday, which is conducive for pushing \nthe water up to the dunes during high tide. In addition, normal tide \nlevels will also be on the increase due to the new moon. Latest P-\nETSS run indicated that tide levels may exceed the 2ft MSL threshold \nduring high tide Monday afternoon with with up to 2.5 feet above MSL \npossible. The caveat is that models keep the long period swells at 7 \nseconds or less, but this may be under-estimated. Will continue to \nmonitor trends for any changes to the tide levels and swells.",
